I don't think it's reasonable to expect any more or less objective
definition to include "doesn't suck" either as a criterion or as an
implication.  According to Sturgeon's Law, 90% of everything is crap;
I see no reason not to expect the rule to apply to roguelikes as well.


FWIW, although I'm not sure if there _is_ any generally accepted
definition of "roguelike" other than "bearing at least some vague
resemblance to some aspects of Rogue", the short definition I've
mostly seen quoted is something like "a text adventure played on
gridlike map drawn with ASCII art".  To elaborate on that, I'd set out
the following personal criteria for a game to be called a roguelike:

1. Played on (and typically against) a computer.

2. Playable on a text-only terminal.

3. Turn-based, with few if any real-time aspects.

4. The player mostly controls the actions of a single character.

5. The game area consists of a gridlike map shown on the terminal,
   with each character occupying a single (distinct) square.

The first four criteria define a text adventure game; the fifth
distinguishes roguelikes from other text adventures such as Zork, and
is thus in a sense, to me, the main defining feature of a roguelike.

The first two criteria also serve to exclude tabletop games such as
D&D random dungeons; to me this is mostly a matter of context, insofar
as the term "roguelike" seems to be computer gaming jargon and only
well defined within that field.  The second criterion is phrased such
that it excludes purely graphical games while allowing for roguelikes
with optional graphical interfaces such as the X11 port of Nethack;
this may be somewhat debatable, but seems to have been historically
important to distinguish roguelikes from later graphical adventure
games.  The third criterion excludes real-time action games, while the
fourth excludes strategy games as well as, incidentally, computerized
versions of traditional board games such as chess.

The second half of the last criterion is there to distinguish true
roguelikes from hybrid games that use the map only for long distance
movement, with most of the actual gameplay interaction happening
within single squares.  It also effectively implies a single mostly
consistent length scale and the unification of combat with movement
that was discussed earlier in the thread.

Of course, other defining characteristics could be proposed, such as
the use of single-key commands as opposed to the more verbose commands
of traditional text adventures, the use of randomly generated maps, or
the inability to revert to an earlier save file after death or some
other unwanted event.  I don't personally consider these essential,
though I'd certainly look askance at a "roguelike" that didn't have
any of these.

And yes, by my definition robotfindskitten is indeed a roguelike, even
if, as you note, it is debatable whether it should actually be called
a game.  I don't really see a need to have roguelikes be a strict
subset of games, and in any case tend to favor the broader definitions
of "game" used by mathematicians, where almost anything is a game if
you look at it the right way.


